arch scroll
name Recovered Field Journal ? Darcap Expedition Record
msg
Recovered Field Journal - Darcap Expedition Record
Archivist: Selne Irix, Guild of the Smoking Cauldron (Detached Observer Status)
Location Designation: Afterlife, Peripheral Zone Beyond Return Vector

Entry begins after first successful non-terminal traversal.

Death, as experienced by the common hero, is mercifully instructional.

The blackened antechamber is consistent across entries: a room without corners, walls absorbing rather than reflecting light. At its center, a single table. Upon it, the instructional volume commonly titled The Handbook for the Recently Deceased. Its language is calm, almost bureaucratic, as if reassurance itself were a function. There is no threat here, only guidance.

Also present: the boat.

It waits without impatience. No oars, yet capable of return. This is the sanctioned exit, the agreed-upon exception to finality. The fog-bound path nearby is not mentioned in the book, which I find significant. What is not explained is often more important than what is.

I chose the fog.

Transition was not immediate. The fog resisted measurement, consuming distance unevenly. Then, without ceremony, I stood in the Infinite Graveyard.

The name is insufficient. The graveyard is not merely large; it refuses containment. Headstones extend beyond sight, beyond horizon, repeating yet never duplicating. Names appear in languages no longer spoken, scripts half-erased by conceptual erosion rather than weather. Some stones are warm. Others radiate faint hostility. A few whisper, though I cannot yet determine whether this is memory or echo.

Each stone marks a hero who died. Not all were great. Not all were remembered. The graveyard does not discriminate. It records.

Movement here is unreliable. Cardinal directions exist only by convention. I tested pacing: ten steps north does not equal ten steps south. Paths bend around absence rather than terrain. Certain landmarks recur; the lamp, the bell-post, the broken arch, yet revisiting them does not guarantee proximity.

One particularly troubling account, corroborated by two separate explorers, describes a method of displacement: passing the lamp south five times, then turning right. No compass is used. "South" is defined by the lamp's shadow, which should not exist. Upon completion, the explorer found the Lost Temple of Gorokh standing behind them, intact, as if it had always been there.

I attempted the sequence. On the fourth pass, I felt observed?not by entities, but by the graveyard itself, as though it were checking my syntax.

I did not complete the fifth.

I am increasingly convinced that the Infinite Graveyard is not merely a repository of the dead, but a routing system. It stores not just remains, but paths, and some of those paths lead elsewhere; perhaps back into the world, perhaps into places erased or deferred.

Why Darcap is interested in this should be obvious. Artifacts do not always originate in life. Some may be retrieved from death, provided one knows how to walk incorrectly.

I will return while the boat still answers me. Further excursions will require better rules, or the abandonment of rules altogether.

End entry.
endmsg
