arch scroll_2
name Field Research Log of Istram Voln
msg
Field Research Log of Istram Voln,
Licensed Adventurer of Darcap, Guild-Independent

Southern Ice Expanse, Uncharted Reach
Seventeenth Day Beyond the Last Marked Route

I write this with fingers stiff and uncooperative, ink thickening as if it, too, resents the cold. If this entry survives, let it be known my intent was observation, not heroics. Darcap values results, but it also values the living return of those it sends forth.

The southern ice is not empty. That is the first correction I must make to the maps. It is occupied by pressure, by patience, by an intelligence that does not announce itself. Even silence here has mass.

I came to study dragons as phenomena rather than legends. Our guilds traffic in artifacts attributed to them, in alchemical residues scraped from old wars, in bones and scales bought at great cost and greater uncertainty. None of that prepares one for proximity.

I saw the dragon as a bending of snowfall, a flaw in the idea of distance. When it emerged, sound ceased to function correctly. My wards flared in confusion rather than defense, and I understood, too late, that this creature did not contest space. It authored it.

I raised my hammer by instinct.

Frostbound Conviction has been my constant for years. Forged in Darcap, layered with cold bound to obedience, it answers readily to force and purpose. Stone yields to it. Heat retreats. Even hostile magic slows, crystallizes, waits. I trusted it as one trusts gravity.

The dragon did not strike me. It struck the ground, and the world broke its sentence mid-word. Ice shattered, folded, re-formed. I was thrown clear, breath ripped from me, wards collapsing like poorly argued theories.

I ran.

I do not embellish that. I ran because to remain was to be edited out of the landscape.

The hammer tore from my grip as I fell, skidding across the ice, its enchantments flaring reflexively. I scrambled upright and fled, boots slipping, lungs burning, every step negotiated. Only once did I look back, and that was enough to mark me.

The dragon stood over the hammer. It did not hurry. The artifact's cold answered the greater cold present, radiating outward in familiar geometry, sigils I knew by heart, patterns I had trusted. Then the ice changed its mind.

The hammer did not shatter. It did not resist. It was taken into context.

Ice climbed it smoothly, silently, layer upon layer, not trapping but incorporating. The glow faded. The sigils vanished. What remained was a perfect block of blue-white ice, clear enough that I could still see the outline of the weapon inside, suspended as if awaiting instruction.

The dragon turned away.

I do not know whether it claimed the hammer, neutralized it, or merely set it aside as one might set aside a thought not yet required. I marked the location as best I could, though landmarks here shift with mood and weather.

If someone finds this log and seeks the hammer, understand this: it is not broken, only waiting. The ice around it is not hostile. It is patient. It does not radiate warding, nor curse, nor hunger. It simply holds.

I suspect, though I lack the instruments now, that the enchantment remains intact beneath the ice, accessible through the simplest means, provided one approaches without arrogance or excessive ritual. Cold here respects plain intent.

I will return to Darcap with fewer tools and more certainty than I prefer. The guilds will ask for conclusions. This is mine:

Artifacts do not always fall when lost. Sometimes they are set down by the world itself.

If you stand before the ice and see the hammer within, do not overthink the problem. Heat is not always an enemy of cold. Sometimes it is only a question.

I end this entry now, as my ink is freezing and the wind has begun to circle in a way I no longer find coincidental. If I do not survive the return, let this stand as both warning and invitation.

- Istram Voln
endmsg
