arch book_red
name The City That Refused to Starve
msg
Many cities boast of their founding. Darcap speaks instead of its endurance.

In the year 2264 EK, when the Kingdom of the Masts still believed the sea could bind distant lands together, they founded Darcap at the northern edge of the continent. It was intended as a colony, a place of trade and future autonomy. It never reached that future. In 2287 EK, the Kingdom fell, and Darcap was left behind like a forgotten outpost at the end of a broken sentence.

The sea became an enemy. The land was unforgiving. Crops failed. Game vanished. Hunger arrived quietly, then stayed.

Those early centuries defined Darcap more than any banner or ruler. Many died. Some fled by foot or fragile boat, chasing rumors of better lands. None returned. Whether they perished, were taken, or simply chose never to look back remains unknown. Darcap learned early that survival would not come from rescue.

It came instead from belief.

A colonist named Andreas, sharp-tongued, endlessly hungry, and possessed of a grim sense of humor, began preaching devotion to the Devourers. Their followers, he claimed, would never know hunger again. This was not metaphor. Those who converted endured famine with uncanny resilience.

The city split along a terrible logic. If many converted, food would last longer for those who did not. Thus, both belief and disbelief became survival strategies. Darcap did not become wholly devout, nor wholly resistant. It became divided, negotiated, pragmatic.

After Andreas' death, he was called Andreas the Ravenous. The title was affectionate, if bleak. The church that formed around his teachings bore his name, and with it a truth Darcap has never denied: that the Devourers are real, and that their gifts are costly, but effective.

Today, Darcap is governed not by priests, but by guilds. Yet the hunger that birthed the faith still shapes the city?s bones. Adventurers should understand this before judging what they see. Darcap does not revere death. It remembers starvation?and chose never to kneel before it again.
endmsg
